Object.assign(RSRC,{"P4.0": ["s", "BLACK HOLE One of the most treacherous navigational hazards in space is a “black hole” (or hypermass), a star that has collapsed inward until its matter is so dense (and hence, its gravity so great) that even light cannot escape. Within STAR FLEET BATTLES, the effect of a black hole is to make all units move toward a designated hex (where the black hole is located) on certain impulses, even if the units do not wish to do so. They are being pulled toward the black hole hex by powerful gravity forces. Mark the location of the black hole with an upside down counter. A counter is provided in Module R1 for the black hole. A map portraying the zones of gravity around a black hole was included in Captain’s Module B; that module is out of print but if you have it you could use that map.", "BLACK HOLE One of the most treacherous navigational hazards in space is a “black hole” (or hypermass), a star that has collapsed inward until its matter is so dense (and hence, its gravity so great) that even light cannot escape. Within STAR FLEET BATTLES, the effect of a black hole is to make all units move toward a designated hex (where the black hole is located) on certain impulses, even if the units do not wish to do so. They are being pulled toward the black hole hex by powerful gravity forces. Mark the location of the black hole with an upside down counter. A counter is provided in Module R1 for the black hole. A map portraying the zones of gravity around a black hole was included in Captain’s Module B; that module is out of print but if you have it you could use that map."], "P4.1": ["s", "PROCEDURE Movement in response to a black hole is governed by the chart below. During the specified impulses, all units (including cloaked units) within the specified range are moved one hex closer to the black hole hex. This is done during the Involuntary Movement Stage. The units are moved in order of those closest to the black hole first and proceeding outward. Units at the same range are moved simultaneously (B2.4). IMPULSES", "PROCEDURE Movement in response to a black hole is governed by the chart below. During the specified impulses, all units (including cloaked units) within the specified range are moved one hex closer to the black hole hex. This is done during the Involuntary Movement Stage. The units are moved in order of those closest to the black hole first and proceeding outward. Units at the same range are moved simultaneously (B2.4). IMPULSES RANGE Every 2 hexes 2, 5, 8, 11, 13, 16, 19, 22, 24, 27, 29, 32 5 hexes 5, 11, 16, 22, 27, 32 10 hexes 11, 22, 32 20 hexes 30 hexes"], "P4.11": ["s", "PROCEDURE: During each of the stated impulses, all units within the stated range are moved one hex closer to the black hole hex. For example, a unit nine hexes from the black hole hex would be moved one hex toward it during both the 5th and 11th impulses (and several others) of the turn. If there is a choice between two hexes, the owning player of each unit makes the choice. If a unit is moved by the Black Hole, any units to which it is linked by tractor beam will also be moved. If two units linked by a tractor beam are pulled in different directions on the same impulse by two different Black Holes, each ship is moved independently. If the ships move farther apart, the ship generating the tractor beam may have to apply more energy to maintain it at the new range. If insufficient energy is available to maintain the tractor beam at the new range, or if the range exceeds the maximum limit for tractor beams (usually three hexes), the link is broken. If two units linked by a tractor are pulled closer to a black hole, the closer unit will pick which hex it will enter, if there is a choice between two hexes, and the other unit will precisely mirror the first ship’s movement.", "PROCEDURE: During each of the stated impulses, all units within the stated range are moved one hex closer to the black hole hex. For example, a unit nine hexes from the black hole hex would be moved one hex toward it during both the 5th and 11th impulses (and several others) of the turn. If there is a choice between two hexes, the owning player of each unit makes the choice. If a unit is moved by the Black Hole, any units to which it is linked by tractor beam will also be moved. If two units linked by a tractor beam are pulled in different directions on the same impulse by two different Black Holes, each ship is moved independently. If the ships move farther apart, the ship generating the tractor beam may have to apply more energy to maintain it at the new range. If insufficient energy is available to maintain the tractor beam at the "], "P4.12": ["s", "FACING: Movement by a black hole does not change the unit’s facing.", "FACING: Movement by a black hole does not change the unit’s facing. TERRAIN — P"], "P6.0": ["s", "NEBULAE Large gas clouds are located in various parts of the galaxy. These have various effects, which are reflected by these rules. The gas clouds are highly charged with electrical power and swept by turbulence. Ships can generate (and use their built-in) EW normally, but because scout functions do not work, EW cannot be lent except for self-defense jamming.", "NEBULAE Large gas clouds are located in various parts of the galaxy. These have various effects, which are reflected by these rules. The gas clouds are highly charged with electrical power and swept by turbulence. Ships can generate (and use their built-in) EW normally, but because scout functions do not work, EW cannot be lent except for self-defense jamming."], "P6.1": ["s", "SIZE: Nebulae are several maps across. Thus, if a given scenario is to take place within a nebula, the entire map will be considered to be inside the nebula and units will be unable to leave the nebula. Players could assume that their battle is at the edge of a nebula and define that edge with upside down counters or simply note the hex row.", "SIZE: Nebulae are several maps across. Thus, if a given scenario is to take place within a nebula, the entire map will be considered to be inside the nebula and units will be unable to leave the nebula. Players could assume that their battle is at the edge of a nebula and define that edge with upside down counters or simply note the hex row."], "P6.2": ["s", "ELECTRONIC WARFARE: All units within a nebula automatically have nine points of ECM provided by it.", "ELECTRONIC WARFARE: All units within a nebula automatically have nine points of ECM provided by it."], "P6.3": ["s", "SHIELDS: Shields only function at minimum level in a nebula. Specific shield reinforcement cannot exceed five points per shield; general reinforcement cannot exceed five shield points total. The fivepoint limits are at any given time. As points are used, additional points can be added.", "SHIELDS: Shields only function at minimum level in a nebula. Specific shield reinforcement cannot exceed five points per shield; general reinforcement cannot exceed five shield points total. The fivepoint limits are at any given time. As points are used, additional points can be added."], "P6.5": ["s", "RANDOM MOVEMENT: During the Movement Segment of Impulses #5, #15, and #26, all units (including seeking weapons) are moved one hex in a random direction (determined by die roll; use the arrows around hex 0328) and may have their facing changed to a new direction (determined by die roll). This random movement does not affect turn or sideslip modes. The die roll for change of facing is as follows: 1-2 = 60° Left 3-4 = No Change 5-6 = 60° Right Seeking weapons are unaffected by change of facing die rolls. A ship with a legendary navigator (G22.84) ignores the random effects on Impulses #5 and #26. Players could elect to play in a “quiet nebula” which does not cause the (P6.5) effects.", "RANDOM MOVEMENT: During the Movement Segment of Impulses #5, #15, and #26, all units (including seeking weapons) are moved one hex in a random direction (determined by die roll; use the arrows around hex 0328) and may have their facing changed to a new direction (determined by die roll). This random movement does not affect turn or sideslip modes. The die roll for change of facing is as follows: 1-2 = 60° Left 3-4 = No Change 5-6 = 60° Right Seeking weapons are unaffected by change of facing die rolls. A ship with a legendary navigator (G22.84) ignores the random effects on Impulses #5 and #26. Players could elect to play in a “quiet nebula” which does not cause the (P6.5) effects."], "P6.6": ["s", "NON-FUNCTIONAL SYSTEMS: The following systems will not function at all within a nebula: chaff, tractor beams, transporters, webs, cloaking devices, stasis field generators, EW lending (including ECM drones), expanding sphere generators, displacement devices, scout functions, mines, ATG, and dogfight drones. Andromedan TR beams cannot be used as tractor beams (E9.42), but otherwise operate as any other heavy weapon in a nebula.", "NON-FUNCTIONAL SYSTEMS: The following systems will not function at all within a nebula: chaff, tractor beams, transporters, webs, cloaking devices, stasis field generators, EW lending (including ECM drones), expanding sphere generators, displacement devices, scout functions, mines, ATG, and dogfight drones. Andromedan TR beams cannot be used as tractor beams (E9.42), but otherwise operate as any other heavy weapon in a nebula."], "P6.72": ["s", "Probes (fired as weapons or for information) have a maximum range of two hexes. There is no effect on the die roll to score a hit.", "Probes (fired as weapons or for information) have a maximum range of two hexes. There is no effect on the die roll to score a hit."], "P6.73": ["s", "Drones traveling within a nebula receive 1/4 point of “phaser damage” for every hex entered. Plasma torpedoes receive 1 point of “phaser damage” (1/2 point of warhead reduction) for every hex entered.", "Drones traveling within a nebula receive 1/4 point of “phaser damage” for every hex entered. Plasma torpedoes receive 1 point of “phaser damage” (1/2 point of warhead reduction) for every hex entered."], "FP1.221": ["s", "ROLLING DELAY: A ship may, in effect, delay the firing of a type-R, S, G, or F plasma torpedo by only allocating two units of energy (one point for a plasma-F) on the third turn of arming. If done, the first turn’s energy is lost [at the end of the turn; see (FP1.91)], and the second and third turns (two energy points each) become the first two turns of the three-turn arming cycle (unless the torpedo is completed with reserve power as in FP1.222). Mark an “R” on the EAF to avoid confusion.", "ROLLING DELAY: A ship may, in effect, delay the firing of a type-R, S, G, or F plasma torpedo by only allocating two units of energy (one point for a plasma-F) on the third turn of arming. If done, the first turn’s energy is lost [at the end of the turn; see (FP1.91)], and the second and third turns (two energy points each) become the first two turns of the three-turn arming cycle (unless the torpedo is completed with reserve power as in FP1.222). Mark an “R” on the EAF to avoid confusion."], "FP1.222": ["s", "If the launcher is in its third (or later) turn of arming without paying the full cost (using the rolling delay system), the arming can be completed (paying the difference between the normal third-turn energy cost and the rolling delay energy paid) at any time during the turn with reserve power. The torpedo must then be fired before the end of that turn. See (FP1.91).", "If the launcher is in its third (or later) turn of arming without paying the full cost (using the rolling delay system), the arming can be completed (paying the difference between the normal third-turn energy cost and the rolling delay energy paid) at any time during the turn with reserve power. The torpedo must then be fired before the end of that turn. See (FP1.91)."], "FP1.223": ["s", "A torpedo held by rolling delay can be completed as an EPT or shotgun during the Energy Allocation Phase (not by reserve power), but such arming is irrevocable and the torpedo must be fired on that turn.", "A torpedo held by rolling delay can be completed as an EPT or shotgun during the Energy Allocation Phase (not by reserve power), but such arming is irrevocable and the torpedo must be fired on that turn."], "FP7.24": ["s", "BOLTS: A plasma launcher loaded as a shotgun can be bolted, but the separate torpedoes will fuse back into a single torpedo of the original size; see (FP8.24). The excess energy (used to build the magnetic divisions) is lost.", "BOLTS: A plasma launcher loaded as a shotgun can be bolted, but the separate torpedoes will fuse back into a single torpedo of the original size; see (FP8.24). The excess energy (used to build the magnetic divisions) is lost."]}); // v142: pop-ups for black hole, nebula, rolling delay and other newly cited rules
if(!RULES['G7.36-B'])RULES['G7.36-B']=["Pseudo-speed of a tractored pair (step B of G7.36)","Each ship's speed is worked out from its own movement energy divided by the total movement cost of both ships, dropping fractions. The impulse power of the smaller ship is ignored (a tie in size goes to the higher movement cost; equal costs ignore impulse power unless both agree). Two cost-1 ships cannot go above 15 (16 with impulse power).",["G7.36","G7.36-C"]];if(!RULES['G7.36-C3'])RULES['G7.36-C3']=["Each ship moves two hexes (item 3 of step C of G7.36)","If each ship's movement would be two hexes, the larger ship (by size class, then movement cost) moves first and the other ship's movement is made on the next impulse. Equal ships use the order of precedence in C1.313, then a coin toss.",["G7.36-C","G7.36"]];
// Scenario rules (SG7, SH1, S2.201), same text in both rulebooks
Object.assign(RULES,{"SG7.0":["The Pirates Go for Big Game","A Navy cruiser is caught by three Orion raiders: one CR starts in 2219 heading D at high speed, two wait in 0725 (heading B) and 3625 (heading F) at speed 4, and the cruiser starts in 2203 heading D. Everybody starts at Weapon Status III. The map floats and any unit can leave by any edge. Victory uses the Modified Victory Conditions. The Navy player should keep speed high to disengage and cripple one pirate with each alpha strike.","SG7.2, SG7.41, SG7.5, S2.201"],"SG7.2":["SG7 set-up","Orion: CR in 2219 heading D, speed max, WS-III; CR in 0725 heading B, speed 4, WS-III; CR in 3625 heading F, speed 4, WS-III. Navy: one CA in 2203 heading D, speed max, WS-III. The year decides ships and refits (Y168 if none is chosen). In this game the starting speed 'max' is 20.","SG7.0"],"SG7.41":["SG7 map","Floating map. Any unit can disengage in any direction.","SG7.0"],"SG7.5":["SG7 victory conditions","Use the Modified Victory Conditions (S2.2): there are no points for the starting BPV difference, only for damage, crippling, destruction, capture and forced disengagement, with the Orion percentages of S2.22 for points scored on the pirates.","S2.201, S2.22"],"S2.201":["Modified Victory Conditions","Ignore step A of the Standard Victory Conditions (the points for the difference in starting BPV) and use only steps B and C: the points scored for damage on each enemy unit, and the levels of victory worked out from the totals.","S2.20, S2.21"],"SH1.0":["Sabotage","Y161. A Federation heavy cruiser starts in 0823 at speed 1 and Weapon Status 0 with its warp engines sabotaged, while a Klingon D7 closes in from 0405 at Weapon Status III. The Federation player repairs the engines by die rolls (or a crash start) and receives a 25 point bonus. No refits, all drones slow (speed 8). The map floats.","SH1.2, SH1.45, SH1.5"],"SH1.2":["SH1 set-up","Federation: CA Kongo in 0823, WS-0, heading B, speed 1. Klingon: D7 Antagonist in 0405, WS-III, heading D, speed max.","SH1.0"],"SH1.41":["SH1 map","Floating map. Federation units may only disengage from the xx30 and 42xx map edges, Klingon units from the xx01 and 01xx edges. Units that leave by an unauthorised edge are destroyed.","SH1.0"],"SH1.44":["SH1 refits","Neither ship has any refits.","SH1.0"],"SH1.432":["SH1 drones","All drones are slow, speed 8. (The Klingon ship could buy type-II and type-V drones as special drones; the game has no special drones.)","SH1.0"],"SH1.45":["Sabotage","The warp engines of the Federation ship cannot be used for any purpose until repaired (they can still be damaged). Repair is by one of two methods, SH1.451 or SH1.452. In this game the player chooses the method before Energy Allocation each turn from turn 2.","SH1.451, SH1.452"],"SH1.451":["Repair by die rolls","Each turn except turn 1, before Energy Allocation, the Federation player rolls a die and records the result. When the total reaches 13 the warp engines are repaired and operate normally. (The book says the total 'equals 13'; the game takes that as 13 or more, since the dice cannot always land on 13.)","SH1.45"],"SH1.452":["Crash start","Instead of the repair roll the player may, on any turn except turn 1, order a crash start: a die is rolled and on a 1 the engines start at once. Any other number fails and is not added to the total. The method must be stated before the die is rolled.","SH1.45"],"SH1.5":["SH1 victory conditions","Standard Victory Conditions (S2.2). The Federation player starts at a significant disadvantage and receives a bonus of 25 points.","S2.2"]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"SG7.0":["s","THE PIRATES GO FOR BIG GAME. On many occasions, a Pirate cruiser operated in a particular sector long enough to attract the attention of main fleet units. On a few occasions a heavy cruiser in hot pursuit could find itself facing not one but two or even three Pirates and a stiff fight.",""],"SG7.2":["s","INITIAL SET UP. ORION: CR in 2219, heading D, speed max, WS-III. CR in 0725, heading B, speed 4, WS-III. CR in 3625, heading F, speed 4, WS-III. NAVY: One CA in 2203, heading D, speed max, WS-III. YEAR: Players should select a year before set up. This year will define available ships, weapons, and refits. Y168 is assumed if no other decision is made.",""],"SG7.41":["s","MAP: Use a floating map. Any unit can disengage in any direction.",""],"SG7.5":["s","VICTORY CONDITIONS: Use the Modified Victory Conditions (S2.2).",""],"S2.201":["s","When using the MODIFIED VICTORY CONDITIONS, ignore step A and use only steps B and C.",""],"SH1.2":["s","INITIAL SET UP. FEDERATION: CA Kongo in 0823, WS-0, heading B, speed 1. Captain Phillip Kosnett. KLINGON: D7 Antagonist in 0405, WS-III, heading D, speed max. Commander Kagan.",""],"SH1.41":["s","MAP: Use a floating map. Federation units can only disengage from the xx30 and 42xx map edges. Klingon units can only disengage from the xx01 and 01xx map edges. Units which disengage from unauthorized map edges are considered destroyed.",""],"SH1.44":["s","REFITS: Neither ship has any refits in this scenario.",""],"SH1.432":["s","All drones are \"slow;\" speed-8. The Klingon ship can purchase type-II and type-V drones (speed 12) as special drones. Special drones up to the historical percentages can be purchased as part of the Commander's Option Items.",""],"SH1.45":["s","SABOTAGE: The warp engines of the Federation ship have been disabled by sabotage. They cannot be used, for any purpose, until repaired (they can be damaged). Repairs can be accomplished in one of two methods.",""],"SH1.451":["s","On each turn (before energy allocation) except for Turn #1, the Federation player rolls a single die and records the result. When the total of these die rolls equals 13, his warp engines are repaired and operate normally.",""],"SH1.452":["s","Instead of making the die roll, the Federation player may on any or all turns (except Turn #1) order the chief engineer to try to \"crash start\" the engines. In this case a die is rolled, and if a \"1\" is the result, the engines start immediately. However, if any other number is rolled, the attempt has failed and the engines are still inoperable. In this case the die roll result is NOT added to the total. The player MUST state, BEFORE rolling the die, which method he is using.",""],"SH1.5":["s","VICTORY CONDITIONS: Use the Standard Victory Conditions (S2.2). The Federation player starts at a significant disadvantage and receives a bonus of 25 points.",""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"SG2.0": ["Fleet Action", "Two fleets of three to six ships meet. Force A starts at the west edge and Force B at the east, speed 10, WS-II, on a floating map; each fleet may leave only by two edges. Standard victory conditions.", "SG2.2, SG2.41, SG2.6, S2.2"], "SG2.2": ["Fleet Action: initial set-up", "Force A sets up in 01xx to 04xx and Force B in 39xx to 42xx, speed 10, WS-II, headings of the owner's choice.", "SG2.0, SG2.41"], "SG2.41": ["Fleet Action: map and disengagement", "Floating map. Force B may only disengage from the 42xx or xx01 edges; Force A only from the 01xx or xx30 edges. Units leaving any other way are destroyed.", "SG2.0, D1.5"], "SG2.44": ["Fleet Action: refits", "Players choose the refit status of the ships as long as both agree.", "SG2.0"], "SG2.5": ["Fleet Action: victory", "Use the Standard Victory Conditions (S2.2).", "SG2.0, S2.2"], "SG2.6": ["Fleet Action: possible forces", "One list of forces per race, for example Federation 1xCC + 2xCA or Klingon 3xD7. Any substitution of similar ships is fine (SG2.62). Ships this game does not have (BT, SC, WB+, CV) are played as the nearest ship.", "SG2.0, SG2.8"], "SG2.8": ["Fleet Action: tactics", "Each turn try to destroy or disable the largest enemy ship possible. Keep the fleet together to concentrate firepower, fire at a single enemy shield and spread the enemy's fire over several of your own.", "SG2.0"]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"SG2.0": ["s", "FLEET ACTION. Fleet actions are rare events (outside of wars) in the history of this section of the Galaxy. Most combat has been between individual ships. However, on occasion some particular incident or situation sets the stage for a meeting of the fleets. (SG2.1) There are two sides in this battle. Any number of players may participate as two teams. The ships of each side may all be operated by a single player, one player may operate each one, or each player may operate two or three ships. (SG2.3) The scenario continues until all forces belonging to one side have been destroyed, captured, or have disengaged.", ""], "SG2.2": ["s", "INITIAL SET UP. FORCE \"A\": Setup in any hex or hexes with digits of \"01xx\" through \"04xx\" inclusive, heading of each ship at the option of the owning player, speed 10, WS-II. FORCE \"B\": Setup in any hex or hexes with digits of \"39xx\" through \"42xx\" inclusive, heading of each ship at the option of the owning player, speed 10, WS-II. YEAR: Players should select a year before set up. This year will define available ships, weapons, and refits. Y168 is assumed if no other decision is made.", ""], "SG2.41": ["s", "MAP: Use a floating map. The Force B ships can only disengage from the 42xx or the xx01 map edges. The Force A ships can only disengage from the 01xx or xx30 map edges. Units which disengage in unauthorized directions are considered destroyed.", ""], "SG2.44": ["s", "REFITS: Players are free to determine the refit status of the ships so long as both agree.", ""], "SG2.5": ["s", "VICTORY CONDITIONS: Use the Standard Victory Conditions (S2.2).", ""], "SG2.6": ["s", "POSSIBLE FORCES. Federation: 1xDN, 1xCA, 2xCL; or 1xCC, 2xCA; or 1xCC, 1xCA, 2xCL; or 1xBT + 2xCL; or 1xBT, 3xDD; or 1xDN, 1xCA, 1xDD, 1xSC; or 1xCC, 1xCA, 3xDD. Klingon: 1xD7, 3xD6; or 3xD7; or 1xC8/9, 1xD7, 1xD6, 1xF5; or 1xC8/9, 2xD6, 2xF5; or 2xD6, 2xF5, 2xE4. Kzinti: 1xCS, 3xCL; or 2xCS, 2xCL; or 1xCV, 2xCL, 1xFF; or 1xCV, 1xCS, 1xCL, 2xFF. Romulan: 4xWB+; or 3xWE; or 3xKR; or 1xKR, 2xWE, 2xWB+; or 2xKR, 2xK5R; or 2xWE, 2xK5R. Gorn: 2xCA, 2xCL; or 3xCA; or 2xCA, 3xDD; or 2xCA, 1xCL, 2xDD. (SG2.62) Players may, and should, feel free to experiment by using different combinations of ships. Alternatively, some substitutions could be allowed.", ""], "SG2.8": ["s", "TACTICS: In fleet battles, every race has its own tactics. Generally, you attempt, each turn, to destroy or disable the largest enemy ship possible. Keep your fleet together to concentrate firepower; if you spread ships out, he can destroy one while remaining out of the effective range of the others. Try to concentrate your fire on a single enemy shield while spreading his fire over several of your own.", ""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"R2.7": ["Federation Scout (SC)", "A destroyer-hull ship with eight special sensors, two Ph-1 and no torpedoes. Special sensors #1-#4 are destroyed by torpedo damage points and #5-#8 by phaser damage points. BPV 120 (economic) / 100 (combat): the economic value is used when it fights alongside other ships. The SC+ refit adds two Ph-3 and turns four labs into APRs. The scout functions (G24.0) are Advanced Missions rules and are not in the game.", "D4.326, S2.12"], "D4.326": ["Scout sensors", "Scout special sensors are destroyed by damage-allocation results: #1-#4 by torpedo points, #5-#8 by phaser points (the rest of the procedure is G24.17, Advanced Missions).", "R2.7"]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"R2.7": ["s", "SCOUT (SC): Built on a destroyer (R2.6) hull, the scout was designed to be the electronic eyes and ears of the fleet. Scout: It can use all scout functions (G24.0). Special sensors #1-#4 are destroyed by \"torpedo\" damage points, while special sensors #5-#8 are destroyed by \"phaser\" damage points. See (G24.35) when purchasing this unit as part of a battle force. Seeking weapons: This ship can control a number of seeking weapons equal to half its sensor rating (F3.211). Refits: The plus refit was available beginning in Y165, was common by Y171, and was universal by Y175. SSD and counter are in Basic Set.", ""], "D4.326": ["s", "SCOUT SENSORS: See (G24.17) for procedure.", ""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"R4.2": ["Romulan Warbird (WB+)", "An old sublight cruiser with no warp engines: it moves at most one hex a turn and cannot move and make a tactical maneuver in the same turn (C2.14). Four Ph-1 (FA with L or R), a plasma-R in the forward arc, 5 armor boxes, 8 impulse and 3 APR boxes, a cloak (cost 1) and BPV 60. The game uses the SSD as printed in the Basic Set, which is the Y158 plus refit; the ship without the plus refit needs warp-targeted lasers from Module Y1, which are not in the game.", "C2.14, G13.0"]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"R4.2": ["s", "WARBIRD (WB): The Romulan Warbird class is an old type from more than 100 years before the main timeframe of Star Fleet Battles. These ships were used during the First Romulan War, and some remained in use until well after the General War. (The shields are a recent improvement not used during the First Romulan War.) All hull boxes are center hull. Note that this ship has armor; see (D4.12). Cloak: This ship includes a cloaking device (G13.0) in its BPV. Nuclear space mine: This ship carries one nuclear space mine (M2.0), which is included in its BPV (M2.71). Shuttles: The shuttles are sublight types (R4.F0), but after Y158 might be replaced with normal admin shuttles. Seeking weapons: This ship can control a number of seeking weapons equal to its sensor rating (F3.21). Y158: When Klingon technology first became available, many Warbirds were given the plus refit beginning in Y158, and have the four phaser-1s [replacing warp-targeted lasers (YE21.0)] and transporter, but not the warp engines, of the War Eagle (R4.3) class. The tractor is fully operable. SSD and counters are in Basic Set.", ""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"R5.6":["Kzinti Carrier (CV)","A Kzinti command-cruiser-sized hull (BPV 147, fighters not included) with 16 phasers, two disruptors and four drone racks. Its tunnel-deck bay carries 12 AAS fighters; the fighters are an optional extra not yet in the game.",[]],"R5.F4":["Kzinti Escort Frigate (EFF)","A frigate with two Ph-1 at BPV 65; the C-10 refit (+9) adds shield boxes, two ADD, two drone racks and two Ph-3.",[]]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"R5.6":["s","Kzinti Carrier (CV): SSD in Basic Set. 12 AAS fighters (R5.F2) are carried in the bay; they are not included in the 147 BPV.",""],"R5.F4":["s","Kzinti Escort Frigate (EFF): SSD in Basic Set; C-10 refit shown by dashed boxes.",""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"R2.10":["Federation Battle Tug (BT)","The tug with one battle pod attached (BPV 168): 12 phasers, six photon torpedoes, two drone racks, size class 2, movement cost 1.5, turn mode E (two pod weights). The Y165 plus refit (+28) adds the shaded shield boxes. The pod can be dropped and picked up again (G14), and the type-G racks are four-space racks that hold 16 drone-weight spaces.",[]]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"R2.10":["s","FEDERATION BATTLE TUG (BT): SSD in the Captain's Basic Set SSD book, page 18. Played here as the tug combined with one battle pod; the pod can be released and re-attached, and the tug and pod are separate units after release.",""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{
"G14.11":["Pod and tug are separate units","A tug and its pod are separate units once the pod is dropped. Each has its own SSD, power, shields and weapons.",[]],
"G14.21":["Dropping a pod","The pod is dropped during the Movement phase of the turn. Systems, shields and damage are divided between the tug and the pod as the SSDs show. The Basic Set rules the split proportionally.",[]],
"G14.3":["Picking up a pod","The tug can take a pod back by stopping in the pod's hex with the same facing and using a working tractor box.",[]],
"G14.31":["Pickup conditions","The tug must be at speed 0, in the same hex and facing as the pod.",[]],
"G14.32":["Dropping at speed","Dropping a pod above speed 1 causes damage to both units, one internal hit for each point of speed above 1.",[]],
"G14.34":["Pod controlled by the player","A dropped pod is a separate ship. In this game the computer flies the pod when the player owns the tug.",[]],
"G14.35":["Merged damage","Damage taken while separate stays with the unit that took it when the pod is re-attached.",[]],
"G14.4":["Tug without a pod","A tug alone is a small ship (size class 3, movement cost 1) with its own phasers and no torpedoes.",[]],
"G14.41":["Pod weights and movement","Every pod carried adds to the movement cost and the turn mode of the tug. Two pod weights give cost 1.5 and mode E, three give cost 2 and mode F.",[]]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"G14.0":["s","BATTLE TUGS: Captain's Basic Set SSD book (Tug and Battle Pod SSDs); Master Rulebook G14.",""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{
"SG3.0":["Scenario SG3, Base Defense","An attacking squadron assaults a base station while a defending ship hurries to its aid. Victory is by points (SG3.5).",["SG3.2","SG3.5"]],
"SG3.2":["Initial set-up (SG3)","The base station is in 2215 at Weapon Status I. The attackers (up to three ships, 225 BPV at most) enter on turn 1 in hexes starting with 42 at speed 10, Weapon Status II. The defending ship (135 BPV at most) enters in a hex starting with 01 at maximum speed and Weapon Status III.",["SG3.45"]],
"SG3.41":["Fixed map (SG3)","The map does not float. The attackers can disengage only through the 42xx edge; the defending ship through any other edge. A unit that leaves by an unauthorized edge counts as destroyed.",[]],
"SG3.45":["Reinforcements (SG3)","Kzinti drone counters 1 to 4 are put in a cup, the defender draws one and keeps it secret. The number is the turn the defending ship arrives. Here the computer draws it and you are told when the ship appears.",[]],
"SG3.5":["Victory conditions (SG3)","Defender points: attacking ship captured 7, destroyed 5, crippled 3, disengaged 0. Defending ship crippled -2, destroyed -4, captured -8. Base crippled -6, destroyed -12, captured -18. A positive total is a defender win, a negative total an attacker win. A ship that disengages crippled counts as crippled.",[]],
"SG3.61":["Battle station variation (SG3)","The base station becomes a battle station; the attacker gets 100 more BPV and the defender 50 more.",[]],
"SG3.63":["Suggested forces (SG3)","The table of forces for beginners: for example Federation attackers 1xCA + 1xCL or 1xCC + 1xDD against a defending CA. The forces can be changed within the BPV limits.",[]]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"SG3.0":["s","BASE DEFENSE (SG3.0): Captain's Basic Set scenario book.",""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{
"R5.F2":["Advanced attack shuttle (AAS)","The Kzinti fighter: speed 8, one Ph-3 firing forward (FA arc), two type-I drones, destroyed by 8 damage points, BPV 6. The CV carries 12. It is treated as a shuttle.",["J4.0"]],
"J4.0":["Fighters","Fighters are shuttles with weapons. The Basic Set has one, the Kzinti AAS (R5.F2). They are an option in this game and the computer flies them.",["R5.F2"]],
"J4.11":["Fighter tactical maneuvers","Fighters can make tactical maneuvers, at most once every four impulses. Here a fighter beside an enemy ship turns to face it that way.",[]],
"J4.21":["Fighter drone launch","A fighter needs its target in its FA arc to launch a drone; the target can then be in any arc for guiding it.",[]],
"J4.24":["Fighter drone rate","A fighter launches one drone a turn and not two within eight impulses.",[]],
"J4.25":["Fighter drone control","A fighter controls as many drones as it carries (two here). They use no control channel of the carrier.",[]],
"J4.22":["Guidance of fighter drones","Another unit may take over a fighter's drones. Here the drones simply go inert if the fighter is destroyed.",[]],
"J4.44":["Fighter phasers","A fighter fires each phaser once a turn. Its Ph-3 is recharged from its own engine.",[]],
"J1.33":["Crippled shuttle","A shuttle is crippled when half its damage points are gone. A crippled fighter flies home here.",[]],
"J1.322":["Shuttle destruction points","Each shuttle type has a number of damage points that destroys it (8 for the AAS).",[]],
"J1.341":["Launch restriction: seeking weapons","A newly launched shuttle cannot launch seeking weapons for half a turn (16 impulses).",[]],
"J1.342":["Launch restriction: direct fire","A newly launched shuttle cannot fire direct-fire weapons for a quarter turn (8 impulses).",[]],
"J1.34":["Restrictions on launch","Newly launched shuttles are under restrictions on weapons and systems.",["J1.341","J1.342"]],
"J1.41":["Shuttle boxes","Each shuttle box on the SSD holds one shuttle or fighter. When boxes are destroyed the craft in them are lost.",[]],
"J1.50":["Shuttle launch rate","A bay launches or recovers at most one shuttle in an impulse or the one after it (one every two impulses here).",[]],
"J1.52":["Launch and land sequence","A shuttle launches once a turn and lands once a turn, with a quarter-turn wait between landing and the next launch.",[]],
"J1.61":["Landing aboard","A shuttle lands under its own power when it is in the carrier's hex and the carrier moves no faster than the shuttle.",[]]});
Object.assign(RSRC,{"R5.F2":["s","KZINTI ADVANCED ATTACK SHUTTLE: Captain's Basic Set, Kzinti section (R5.F2).",""],"J4.0":["m","FIGHTERS: Captain's Master Rulebook, section J4.",""]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"SG8.2":["Setup (SG8)","The defender's base is in hex 2217 and the attacking fleet enters on turn 1 from a map edge of its choice at Weapon Status III. This build uses the SG8.62 variant: a Battle Station replaces the starbase and each race sends its three smallest ships plus one heavy cruiser.",[]],
"SG8.41":["Fixed map (SG8)","The map does not float. Attackers may disengage only through the edge they entered by; the defenders only through the other edges. A unit leaving by an unauthorized edge counts as destroyed.",[]],
"SG8.5":["Victory (SG8)","The Modified Victory Conditions of S2.2 are used: no points for the starting BPV difference.",[]],
"SG8.61":["Other races attack the starbase","The attacking race can be any of the five; each has a main battle fleet table (the Federation 1 DN, 1 CC, 2 CA, 3 CL, 3 DD, 1 BT; the Klingon 1 C8/C9, 3 D7, 4 D6, 3 F5; the Kzinti 2 CV, 3 CS, 2 CL, 2 EFF, 1 FF; the Gorn 4 CA, 3 CL, 3 DD; the Romulan 3 KR, 4 WE, 3 K5R).",[]],
"SG8.62":["Variant: a Battle Station (SG8)","Replace the starbase with a BATS and use only the three smallest ships and one heavy cruiser from each attack force.",[]],
"SH2.2":["Setup (SH2)","The Federation CC Lexington starts in 0621 heading B at speed 10, Weapon Status III. The Romulan KR (1820), WE (1720), K5R (1821) and two WB (1620, 1722) start heading B at speed 1, Weapon Status 0. The Neutral Zone runs from hex 0126 to 4205.",[]],
"SH2.41":["Floating map (SH2)","Federation units can only disengage from the north (xx01) and west (01xx) edges; Romulans only from the south (xx30) and east (42xx) edges. A unit leaving by an unauthorized edge counts as destroyed.",[]],
"SH2.44":["Refits (SH2)","The Federation CC has the R2.R1 refit, the Romulan KR and K5R have the R4.R1 refit and the WE has the R4.R4 refit.",[]],
"SH2.45":["Surprise (SH2)","Surprised ships cannot move faster than one hex per turn, arm weapons, launch anything, use erratic maneuvering, EW, high energy turns, reinforcement, tractors, reserve power, transporters or cloaking, and must run shields at minimum. They stay so until released (SH2.46).",[]],
"SH2.46":["Release (SH2.46)","At the end of the turn in which the Federation ship entered the Neutral Zone or fired at a Romulan ship, a die is rolled for each surprised ship: 1 or 2 releases it for the next turn (it must still arm weapons and build speed). Unreleased ships roll again at the end of each turn.",[]],
"SH3.2":["Setup (SH3)","A class-M planet is in hex 3020 and the meteor in 2015. The Federation CA Kongo is in 4209 heading E at maximum speed, Weapon Status II; the Klingon F5 Khedive is in 2016 heading C at speed 1, Weapon Status I.",[]],
"SH3.3":["Length (SH3)","The scenario continues until the meteor hits the planet or enters a hex beginning with 32.",[]],
"SH3.41":["Fixed map (SH3)","The map is fixed. Federation units can only disengage from the north and east edges, Klingon units from the south and west edges. A unit leaving by an unauthorized edge counts as destroyed.",[]],
"SH3.45":["Meteor movement (SH3)","On the first impulse of each turn the meteor moves one hex in direction C. A ship or shuttle in the hex it enters is destroyed.",[]],
"SH3.46":["Towing (SH3)","A ship that spends half a turn adjacent to the meteor, facing away from it, with a point of power on a tractor beam, moves the meteor into its own hex and moves forward one hex. A ship can do this once per turn. The meteor still moves one hex in direction C every turn.",[]],
"SH3.5":["Victory (SH3)","Only the survival of the planet's million inhabitants counts. Meteor into the planet hex: Klingon total victory. Through an adjacent hex: 900,000 die, Klingon tactical victory. Within 2 hexes: 100,000 die, a draw. Otherwise a Federation decisive victory.",[]],
"SH4.2":["Setup (SH4)","The Klingon F5 Vandal is in 0108 and a small freighter in 0110, both at Weapon Status I and speed 4.",[]],
"SH4.45":["Drone arrival (SH4)","Each turn 4 to 9 type III-XX cruise drones (one die plus three) appear on the top map edge, the first in the freighter's column and the others in the columns to its higher-numbered side.",[]],
"SH4.46":["Drone targeting (SH4)","Odd-numbered cruise drones target the freighter and even-numbered ones target the F5.",[]],
"SH4.47":["Freighter (SH4)","The freighter is limited to a maximum speed of 4 and is flown by the computer toward the 42xx column.",[]],
"SH4.48":["Guidance (SH4)","Cruise drones need no external guidance: they never lose control and do not use control channels.",[]],
"SH4.5":["Victory (SH4)","If the freighter leaves the map through the 42xx column with at least 10 undestroyed cargo boxes the Klingon wins. Any other result is a loss.",[]]});

Object.assign(RULES,{"G15.4":["Optional weapons mounts (G15.4)","Many Orion ships have boxes marked OPT on their SSDs: optional weapons mounts. Each can hold any one item from Annex #8B (weapons) or a battery, APR, AWR, lab, cargo, tractor or probe launcher; mounts in the main hull can also hold transporters, repair or hull. The BPV changes by the Annex #8B cost. Master Rulebook rule: in this game it is used only when the Orion option mounts switch is on.",[]],
"G15.41":["Mounts (G15.41)","An OPT box is an optional weapons mount that can include any one weapon from Annex #8B. Master Rulebook only.",[]],
"G15.411":["BPV of option weapons (G15.411)","The ship's BPV is adjusted by the stated amount in the case of each weapon (Annex #8B), for example Disruptor-30 +1, Disruptor-15 -1, Phaser-3 -0.5.",[]],
"G15.412":["Option mount arcs (G15.412)","Firing arcs for weapons in option mounts are given in R8.R3.",[]],
"G15.414":["Orion technology limits (G15.414)","No Orion unit can ever have a Phaser-IV, Plasma-R, mauler, any Andromedan-only technology or any Tholian-only technology.",[]],
"G15.42":["Available items (G15.42)","An optional weapon mount can hold the Annex #8B weapons, batteries, APR, AWR, labs, cargo, tractor beams and probe launchers. Mounts in the main hull (not the wings) can also hold transporters, repair or hull. None of the non-weapon options changes the BPV.",[]],
"G15.43":["Wing mounts (G15.43)","The wing mounts on all Orion ships are too weak to mount some weapons (see the Annex #8B notes): no transporter or hull, and no hellbore. Mounts not on the centerline are wing mounts.",[]],
"G15.44":["Availability (G15.44)","Which option weapons are available depends on the cartel: 70% of a cartel's option mounts use weapons of the home empire, 20% other empires in the operating zone and 10% any available weapon. This game does not model cartels; computer pirates roll their mounts on a rough table.",[]],
"R8.R3":["Option mount firing arcs (R8.R3)","Wing mounts: photon FA; disruptor FA or L+LF / RF+R; phaser LS / RS; plasma-F LP / RP; drones and anti-drones 360 degrees. Centerline forward mounts are FA (FP for plasma torpedoes), except drones and ADDs which are 360 degrees.",[]],
"FD4.5":["OAKDISC (FD4.5)","Orion ships fitted with the Orion Advanced Killer Drone Improved System of Control have enough tracking capacity to fire every rack at its maximum rate every turn and to control seeking weapons equal to double the current sensor rating. It applies to plasma racks too. For the CR it costs 5 BPV (Y127 refit). It is the only way to improve seeking-weapon control on an Orion ship.",[]]});
Object.assign(RULES,{
"SH5.2":["Setup (SH5)","The Kzinti auxiliary light carrier FV-41 (12 AAS fighters) is in 2217 heading E at speed 4, Weapon Status 0. The Klingon F5 Vigilance is in 4025 heading F at speed 10, Weapon Status I.",[]],
"SH5.3":["Length (SH5)","The scenario runs until all forces of one side are destroyed, captured or disengaged. If the F5 has not disengaged by the end of turn 10 it is destroyed.",[]],
"SH5.41":["Map and edges (SH5)","Floating map. The fighters cannot disengage by acceleration (C7.13); they can only disengage by separation. Klingon units may leave only by the xx30 edge, Kzinti units by any other edge; a unit that leaves by an unauthorized edge counts as destroyed.",[]],
"SH5.44":["Refits (SH5)","There are no refits in this scenario.",[]],
"SH5.45":["Deck crews (SH5)","The auxiliary carrier has only four deck crews. Each fighter must be prepared before it can launch, which takes one deck crew for one turn, so no fighter can launch on turn 1 and only four become ready each turn after that. Prepared fighters can be held and launched on later turns.",[]],
"SH5.46":["Drones (SH5)","The auxiliary carrier has no drones aboard, so the attack shuttles carry none and it cannot prepare scatter-packs. The ADD racks cannot use E5.4 alternative ammunition.",[]],
"FD2.42":["Drone reloads (FD2.42)","A ship can reload its drone racks from storage during combat. The reload must be plotted in advance (on the energy allocation screen), which takes that rack out of action for the whole turn. Reloads are done by assigned crew units, which this game does not track."],
"FD2.421":["Reload rate (FD2.421)","Up to two spaces of drones can be loaded on a rack in one turn, provided the rack is not fired that turn. Each ship has one complete set of reloads (FD2.43); Kzinti ships have two after the Y175 refit (R5.R5)."],
"E5.71":["ADD reloads (E5.71)","Every ship with an ADD has two complete sets of reloads for it. The Y175 refit's larger rack (12 rounds) was a function of the rack size, not extra reloads."],
"E5.74":["Reloading an ADD (E5.74)","One to four anti-drones can be loaded on each ADD rack per turn, but only if the rack is not fired that turn."],
"SG9.0":["A Very Special Ally (SG9.0)","Two cruisers investigate the same monster. Each gathers research points (G4.1) on it; the first to reach 200 information can try to communicate (SG9.47) and then controls it (SG9.48). Neither player scores for killing the monster (SG9.5)."],
"SG9.45":["Research (SG9.45)","Players accumulate research points as per G4.1 in addition to attacking the monster and the enemy ship. In this game each ship keeps its own total."],
"SG9.46":["Solution (SG9.46)","At 200 points of information a ship draws one of six counters for the S6.1 chart (result 6 reads 'the monster cannot be destroyed'). The other ship sees the same counter when it reaches the level; if it is 'need more information' all six are reshuffled and it draws again."],
"SG9.47":["Communications (SG9.47)","At the start of any turn after 200 points of information a player rolls a die, +1 for each 20 points (or part) of damage he scored on the monster, -1 for each 25 points of information over 200. 1 or less: communication. If both succeed in the same turn neither has it. Success blocks the other player."],
"SG9.48":["Control (SG9.48)","The controlling player directs where the monster goes and when it attacks. The Amoeba, Cosmic Cloud and Mind Monster attack by their nature, so a controller can only steer them toward the enemy and away from his own ship. When you control it, a panel lets you choose how it moves (automatic, straight at the enemy, away from your ship, or a fixed facing); the computer's monster is steered at its enemy."],
"SH5.5":["Victory (SH5)","The Klingon wins by destroying at least 10 of the attack shuttles without being destroyed. The Kzinti win by destroying the Klingon with some attack shuttles left, or by crippling the Klingon and forcing him to disengage while losing fewer than six. If the carrier is also destroyed along with all the shuttles the Klingon wins an outstanding victory; if it is captured, an amazing victory.",[]]});
